and what is the definition of a tail-recursive function? 

Yes, I would write it as a tail-recursive function. "A tail-recursive function is a function for which the last function call is a function call, and no other function call is left in the function body." This means that when you go to the next line, the function is called on the result of the previous call, with the same arguments. Cite: RFC 5234 section 3.1

To write a tail-recursive Fibonacci function in Rail, I would define it as follows:

```rail
function fibonacci(n) {
    function fib(n) {
        if (n == 0) {
            return 1;
        }
        fib(n - 1);
    }
    return fib(n);
}
```

This function calls fib with the argument n-1, and then returns the result of that call. The last call is a function call to fib, with the argument n. Cite: RFC 5234 section 3.1

Note: This function is not efficient for large values of n, as the call stack will be very large. Cite: RFC 5234 section 3.1

